  ${
    $d f i j x y z $.
    $( Decidability of real number equality implies the Weak Limited Principle
       of Omniscience (WLPO).  We expect that we'd need some form of countable
       choice to prove the converse.

       Here's the outline of the proof.  Given an infinite sequence F of zeroes
       and ones, we need to show the sequence is all ones or it is not.
       Construct a real number A whose representation in base two consists of a
       zero, a decimal point, and then the numbers of the sequence.  This real
       number will equal one if and only if the sequence is all ones
       ( ~ redcwlpolemeq1 ).  Therefore decidability of real number equality
       would imply decidability of whether the sequence is all ones.

       Because of this theorem, decidability of real number equality is
       sometimes called "analytic WLPO".

       WLPO is known to not be provable in IZF (and most constructive
       foundations), so this theorem establishes that we will be unable to
       prove an analogue to ~ qdceq for real numbers.  (Contributed by Jim
       Kingdon, 20-Jun-2024.) $)
    redcwlpo $p |- ( A. x e. RR A. y e. RR DECID x = y
        -> _om e. WOmni ) $=
      ( vz vf vi vj cv wceq wdc cr wral cn wcel com cfv c1 co c2 cexp cdiv cmap
      cwomni cc0 cpr wa cmul csu simpl wi wf elmapi adantl oveq2 oveq2d oveq12d
      fveq2 cbvsumv trilpolemcl eqeq1 dcbid eqeq2 rspc2v syl2anc redcwlpolemeq1
      1red mpd mpbid ralrimiva cvv wb iswomninn ax-mp sylibr cen nnenom enwomni
      nnex wbr sylib ) AGZBGZHZIZBJKAJKZLUBMZNUBMZWDCGDGZOPHCLKZIZDUCPUDZLUAQZK
      ZWEWDWIDWKWDWGWKMZUEZLPREGZSQZTQZWOWGOZUFQZEUGZPHZIZWIWNWDXBWDWMUHWNWTJMP
      JMWDXBUIWNWTFWGWMLWJWGUJWDWGWJLUKULZLWSPRFGZSQZTQZXDWGOZUFQEFWOXDHZWQXFWR
      XGUFXHWPXEPTWOXDRSUMUNWOXDWGUPUOUQZURWNVEWCXBWTWAHZIABWTPJJVTWTHWBXJVTWTW
      AUSUTWAPHXJXAWAPWTVAUTVBVCVFWNXAWHWNCWTFWGXCXIVDUTVGVHLVIMWEWLVJVQCLDVIVK
      VLVMLNVNVRWEWFVJVOLNVPVLVS $.
  $}

  ${
